narrative:
## More Than One Message

In your first program, you printed a single message. But real programs say many things! Just like you'd write several lines on a party sign, your program can print one message after another.

```xiom
fn main() {
  io.println("Hi there!");
  io.println("Welcome to coding.");
  io.println("Let's have some fun.");
}
```

Each call to `io.println` prints its message on a new line. The computer reads your code from top to bottom and runs each instruction in that exact order — every single time.

### Printing Numbers

Here's something cool: `io.println` isn't just for text. You can print numbers too, without any quotes around them:

```xiom
fn main() {
  io.println("I am");
  io.println("12");
  io.println("years old.");
}
```

When you run this, each piece appears on its own line:

```
I am
12
years old.
```

### Text vs Numbers — What's the Difference?

Notice that `"I am"` has quotes but `12` doesn't. That's because text (called **strings**) needs quotes to say "this is words, not code." Numbers are numbers — the computer already knows what they are. If you put quotes around a number like `"12"`, you're telling the computer "treat this as text," and it won't be able to do math with it later.

### How The Computer Thinks

It helps to picture the computer as a very obedient helper. It reads each line and does exactly what you say — no more, no less. If you tell it to print three things, it prints three things. If you change the order, it changes the order. The computer never guesses or gets creative; it just follows your instructions, top to bottom.

